Findings
  B7-L1 (blocking) Prove-it is not implemented as specified. The spec: the tool
        presents a problem from the corpus, takes the owner's answer and
        verifies it. The CLI verifies claims the user types in full (`--claim`,
        `--identity`, stdin) or replays a fixture where problem and answer are
        both pre-written (`--session`, `--test-session`). Nothing presents a
        problem and nothing reads mathd/corpus/.
  B7-L2 (blocking) There is no explicit reveal command. The spec requires one,
        separate from Prove-it.
  B7-L3 (blocking) The ledger records only Failed claims, and an entry has no
        problem id, no verdict and no time taken. The spec: log every attempt
        with problem id, answer given, verdict, timestamp, time taken. JSON
        lines instead of SQLite is the lead's proposal and stands until the
        owner rules.
  B7-L4 (blocking) The independent oracle mathd/tests/check_b7_ledger.py
        (CPython json + hashlib) is not run by any workflow or script. CI runs
        only cargo test, so the hand-written SHA-256 and JSON escaping are
        checked in CI only by their own crate.
  B7-L5 (owner) The done-when is the owner doing twenty problems. Agents cannot
        close it; it needs the owner's session transcript.


5. K4: freebuff FAIL accepted, and a correction
-----------------------------------------------

docs/reviews/k4-freebuff.log ends:

  $ sed -n 277,280p docs/reviews/k4-freebuff.log
  VERDICT: FAIL (constants and algorithms verified against OpenSSL/hashlib/RFC
  8032; 2 of the 4 required plants still exit 33, so the gate does not detect
  non-canonical-S malleability or an all-zero public key, and the verifier has no
  caller in the boot path although its module doc claims enforcement)

The lead accepts the verdict and findings F1-F5 as written.

Correction. My PROGRESS-REPORT.md draft of 2026-10-24 said the kernel's Ed25519
checker "accepts a malleable signature and an all-zero public key". That is
wrong. freebuff's F2 says the code's behaviour is correct, and F4 says the host
harness shows all 12 fixture negatives are rejected. The defect is the gate: a
broken checker would pass it. The draft has been corrected (uncommitted, owner's
file).

Required for K4 (antigravity):
  - F1, F2, F4: the in-kernel gate runs all 12 negatives per vector from
    tests/fixtures/gen_ed25519_openssl.py's output, including S + L and R with
    y >= p, plus the all-zero public key. Re-run freebuff's four plants
    (docs/reviews/k4-freebuff-vectors.py and the p1-p4 edits in section 4 of
    the log); all four must exit 35.
  - F5: pin the 448-bit and 896-bit messages and the 112/128-byte padding
    boundary for SHA-256 and SHA-512 in the gate.
  - F3: the trust.rs module doc claims enforcement that does not exist. Change
    the doc to say "no caller yet". Wiring the verifier into ELF load belongs
    with the ring 3 work, not this fix.
  - Do not touch the verifier's arithmetic unless a plant shows it is wrong.


Appendix A: plants.py (R5)
--------------------------

import subprocess, sys, os
P = [
 ("P1 prompt budget disabled", "src/novacore/prompts.py", "PROMPT_BUDGET = 24_000", "PROMPT_BUDGET = 24_000_000", "test_prompts"),
 ("P2 event_id back to microsecond timestamp", "src/novacore/capabilities.py", 'f"stub-{uuid4().hex}"', "f\"stub-{datetime.now(UTC).strftime('%Y%m%dT%H%M%S%fZ')}\"", "test_capabilities"),
 ("P3 search score back to raw overlap", "src/novacore/memory.py", "score = overlap / math.sqrt(len(note_terms))", "score = overlap", "test_memory"),
 ("P4 omission marker dropped", "src/novacore/prompts.py", 'body += f"\\n{OMISSION_MARKER} ({omitted} older entries)"', "pass", "test_prompts"),
]
for name, path, old, new, test in P:
    orig = open(path, "rb").read(); t = orig.decode()
    print("===", name, "| pattern count", t.count(old))
    if t.count(old) != 1: continue
    open(path, "wb").write(t.replace(old, new).encode())
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, "-m", "unittest", "tests." + test], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120, env=dict(os.environ, PYTHONPATH="src"))
        fails = [l for l in r.stderr.splitlines() if l.startswith(("FAIL:", "ERROR:"))]
        print("  exit", r.returncode, "|", r.stderr.strip().splitlines()[-1], "|", "CAUGHT" if r.returncode else "NOT CAUGHT")
        for l in fails[:3]: print("  ", l)
    finally:
        open(path, "wb").write(orig); print("  restored:", open(path, "rb").read() == orig)


